# Handicraft API — Docker Compose secrets and tuning.
#
# Usage:
#   cp .env.example .env
#   chmod 600 .env          # the file holds two secrets
#   $EDITOR .env            # fill in the two REQUIRED values
#   docker compose up -d --build
#
# docker compose reads `.env` from this directory automatically; it never needs
# to be passed on the command line. `.env` is gitignored, so the secrets stay
# out of version control — which is exactly why they live here instead of being
# hardcoded in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Generate a strong value with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Use -hex, NOT -base64. The Redis password is embedded in a URL
# (redis://:PASSWORD@redis:6379), and the base64 alphabet contains "/" and "+",
# where "/" terminates the userinfo component and breaks URL parsing. A base64
# password almost always contains "/" and will fail to connect. Hex is
# alphanumeric, so it is URL-safe.
#
# The REQUIRED entries below use `${VAR:?...}`, which fails the compose run with
# the message shown when the value is missing OR empty. That is deliberate: the
# alternative is silently starting with a known default password.

# OPTIONAL, and only used if you enable the `postgres` compose profile.
# The default database is SQLite, which needs no separate process and no
# password. PostgreSQL would need ~150-250 MB of RAM on its own, which does not
# fit on a 512 MB VPS — leave this blank unless you have >= 2 GB and have
# uncommented SQL_DSN plus the postgres entry in depends_on.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# REQUIRED. Redis password. Referenced by --requirepass, the healthcheck's
# REDISCLI_AUTH, and REDIS_CONN_STRING.
#
# Do not reuse the Postgres password. Redis here holds real state rather than
# disposable cache: user login sessions, quota reservations for in-flight
# requests, and rate-limit counters.
REDIS_PASSWORD=

# OPTIONAL. Redis connection pool size. Upstream default is 10; raise it under
# high concurrency.
REDIS_POOL_SIZE=10

# OPTIONAL. Redis memory ceiling. Redis runs with --maxmemory-policy noeviction,
# so reaching this limit makes writes fail loudly instead of silently evicting
# sessions and quota state.
#
# 64 MB suits a single-node deployment. This is a ceiling, not a reservation:
# Redis only takes what it actually holds. Do not raise it without checking
# total RAM — the application alone uses 200-300 MB.
REDIS_MAXMEMORY=64mb
